<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Catalog>
    <!-- Debug only. Deliberately a buff rather than a change to the weapon:
         the player-DPS to worker-DPS ratio is the single number P0 exists to
         measure, so retuning the weapon to make testing comfortable would
         quietly destroy the thing being tested. This leaves the real numbers
         alone and can be switched on when the loop needs to be driven fast.

         Expires on its own, and carries an icon on the unit while it is up, so
         a session cannot be measured with it accidentally left running. -->
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_Debug_Haste">
        <Alignment value="Positive"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-stimpack.dds"/>
        <Duration value="600"/>
        <Modification MoveSpeedMultiplier="2" AttackSpeedMultiplier="10"/>
    </CBehaviorBuff>

    <!-- The inner-room stockpile, as a real harvestable node.

         RemoveWhenEmpty is off, which is the whole reason this works: a stock
         mineral field deletes itself when it runs dry, and this one has to
         survive being emptied so clearing more debris can top it back up.
         Capacity is far above anything a session will reach, because the cap
         is not a design number, how fast debris fills it is.

         IdealHarvesterCount is display only, a suggestion shown in the UI; it
         does not change yield. Throughput is HarvestTime against HarvestAmount,
         and those are the two numbers to design. -->
    <CBehaviorResource id="Lob_Pile_Ore">
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\icon-mineral.dds"/>
        <Capacity value="100000"/>
        <Contents value="0"/>
        <HarvestTime value="2.786"/>
        <HarvestAmount value="5"/>
        <Flags index="RemoveWhenEmpty" value="0"/>
        <ReturnDelay value="0.5"/>
        <IdealHarvesterCount value="2"/>
    </CBehaviorResource>

    <!-- Recovery in the rest hall (baseline 8). Applied and removed by script
         according to where the character is and whether the core is still held,
         but the rate itself is data, so a welfare-department upgrade later
         changes a number here rather than any code. -->
    <CBehaviorBuff id="Lob_RestHall_Recovery">
        <Alignment value="Positive"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-scannersweep.dds"/>
        <Modification>
            <VitalRegenArray index="Life" value="12"/>
        </Modification>
    </CBehaviorBuff>

    <!-- ============================================================
         Employee stats.

         The four stats are four CBehaviorVeterancy on the same unit. The XP
         natives address a veterancy by behavior name, so one unit carries four
         independent curves, each with its own level, its own per-level
         Modification and its own UI. A work result is one UnitXPSetCurrentXP
         call and the effect of levelling up needs no script at all — it is the
         Modification rows below.

         That is why the stat's effect belongs HERE and not in Galaxy. Fortitude
         is life, Justice is attack speed, Temperance is work speed, Prudence is
         SP; all four have engine-side homes, so src/galaxy/11_employee.galaxy
         moves the number and never applies its consequence.

         The Modification rows are deliberately empty for now. Two of the four
         targets do not exist yet (there is no SP vital and no work ability),
         and inventing numbers for the other two would put made-up balance in
         the one place nobody would think to look for it later.

         MinVeterancyXP is per-level, not cumulative — stock DehakaVeterancy
         has 100 on every row and levels every 100 XP. So the increments below
         are the differences of the design thresholds:

             I  0    II 30    III 45    IV 65    V 85    EX 100
                      +30       +15       +20      +20      +15

         Every XPFraction is zero, so the ONLY thing that moves a stat is an
         explicit call. Stock CBehaviorVeterancy defaults Kills to 1, which
         would have employees quietly training themselves on zerglings — and
         that is not a bug to avoid, it is Training Team lv2 ("员工和玩家单位
         对虫潮造成伤害时也同时被动提高属性"), so it has to be off until that
         upgrade turns it on. When it does, it raises DamageDealt from here
         through an EffectArray row and needs no script. UnitXPGainEnable is the
         per-unit switch if a finer one is ever wanted.

         CombineXP is off, also against the stock default. It is on for units
         that carry one veterancy, where it cannot matter; these carry four, and
         four curves that share a pool would not be four curves. Worth watching
         in the first test that levels really do move independently.

         from docs/usr/quests_unlocks.md. Galaxy holds those same absolute
         thresholds because the sum rule in agents-and-abnormalities.md 2.4 has
         to be readable next to its justification; if one side is edited the
         other must follow. The default cap is 99, so EX is unreachable until
         something lifts it — that is the design, not an oversight.
         ============================================================ -->
    <CBehaviorVeterancy id="Lob_Stat_Fortitude">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-defensivematrix.dds"/>
        <Flags index="CombineXP" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="Kills" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="DamageDealt" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="DamageTaken" value="0"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="30"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="15"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="20"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="20"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="15"/>
    </CBehaviorVeterancy>
    <CBehaviorVeterancy id="Lob_Stat_Prudence">
        <EditorCategories value="AbilityorEffectType:Units"/>
        <InfoIcon value="Assets\Textures\btn-ability-terran-battlefieldawareness.dds"/>
        <Flags index="CombineXP" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="Kills" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="DamageDealt" value="0"/>
        <XPFraction index="DamageTaken" value="0"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="30"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="15"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="20"/>
        <VeterancyLevelArray MinVeterancyXP="20"/>
